<article class="env-proposition" id="A780C6">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/A780C6">Proposition <span data-tag="A780C6">6.3.4.1</span>.</a>final object!of the $\infty $-category of $\infty $-topoi Let $\calX $ be an $\infty $-topos. Then $\Fun ^{\ast }(\SSet , \calX )$ is a contractible Kan complex. In particular, $\SSet $ is a final object in the $\infty $-category $\RGeom $ of $\infty $-topoi. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We observe that $\SSet \simeq \Shv (\Delta ^0)$ where the $\infty $-category $\Delta ^0$ is endowed with the “discrete” topology (so that the empty sieve does not constitute a cover of the unique object). According to Proposition <a href="/tag/1A989C" data-tag="1A989C">6.2.3.20</a>, the $\infty $-category $\Fun ^{\ast }(\SSet , \calX )$ is equivalent to the full subcategory of $\calX \simeq \Fun (\Delta ^0, \calX )$ spanned by those objects $X \in \calX $ which correspond to left exact functors $\Delta ^0 \rightarrow \calX $. It is clear that these are precisely the final objects of $\calX $, which form a contractible Kan complex (Proposition <a href="/tag/F95E46" data-tag="F95E46">1.2.12.9</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>